{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/60613-compromise-breaking-point-design","id":60613,"title":"Compromise breaking point design","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-29T03:17:08-08:00","id":60613,"title":"Compromise breaking point design","content":"I am confused about the &quot;breaking point&quot; design at character creation for Demons.<br /> <br /> p.113 talks about the five questions but they do not answer what acts as a compromise, but rather what had been a compromise before. And it specifically states that Demon has a lot less flexibility in customising these questions than other lines. Which is consistent with the fact that, as far as I can see, the design of compromise points isn't mentioned in the character creation section.<br /> <br /> But then p. 239 talks about compromises as player defined traits that are a part of character creation.So which is it? And what are the guidelines for designing compromises for your character? Are the three questions on p. 239 the entirety of the guidance in the RAR (What is the character afraid of? What does the character not want to do under any circumstances? What are his Cover\u2019s weaknesses and how could angels exploit them?) or is there more advice elsewhere in the book?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2108-genesis","name":"genesis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T11:51:23-08:00","id":60952,"title":"","content":"That chapter is more for the storyteller though, it's his job to look at the 5 questions that the players answered and come up with ways throughout the story that the answers to those questions could break a characters cover. The three questions you mentioned are for the storyteller, so that he can make a better story","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/991-pgolasze13","name":"pgolasze13","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T00:45:19-08:00","id":62040,"title":"","content":"Character creation references page 113... Specifically the example character creation section where Luke is creating Gabrielle for Lauren's game (pg 86) page 113 continued on 114 also lists &quot;what causes compromise&quot;. <br /> <br /> 239 is part of the Storytelling chapter, specifically setting building and how the ST works with what the characters have presented.<br /> If we examine the sections where each is located and the context it is written about we see that pages 113-114 are authoritative and on point with the mechanics of compromise. What causes compromise and what happens after compromise has occurred. While page 239 discusses compromise very quickly, shallowly and only in the context of setting building and specifically individual characters. <br /> <br /> In regards to page 239 where it describes &quot;player defined traits&quot; I think this refers to &quot;Taking action grossly out of character for your Cover:&quot; which is a cause of compromise discussed on page 114. Read in this context, the players have latitude in determining SOME compromises.<br /> <br /> for example: if my Cover, Ms. Aigh is a new-age life coach whose lived a relatively sedate existance, murder would be a compromise for that specific cover.<br /> However if I had another cover which was a serial killer straight out of criminal minds, murder would not necessarily compromise that cover. However, perhaps murder outside of the serial killer's signature might provoke a compromise roll.<br /> One more example: if one of my covers is a young heir to a fortune, whose life revolves around partying all night and outrageous behaviour and who had never done a kind or charitable thing in their life was to suddenly give away their inheritance and begin to live a life of quiet contemplation, study, reflection and an emphasis on charitable works.... this should provoke a compromise roll.<br /> <br /> so my take is this: 113-114 and 239 can coexist but in case of conflict 113 -114 supersede 239.<br /> <br /> though perhaps 239 could be changed to say &quot;some compromises may be defined by the player at character creation&quot; - shrug-<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1930-rixx","name":"Rixx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1930_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}